  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_consumed_metric / weighting_coefficients
       Q: What relative weights should be applied to labor-hours, kWh, and material kg when aggregating 'resource consumed' — for example, if energy costs 3× more than labor per unit, should the energy weight be proportionally higher, and is there an explicit cap on how much any single resource type (especially energy) can increase relative to baseline even if the overall ratio improves?
       A: Apply equal normalized weights (1/3 each) to labor-hours, kWh, and material kg after min-max normalization against a 30-day rolling baseline; additionally cap energy consumption at +15% above baseline shift average to prevent unbounded power draw in pursuit of a better ratio.
    2. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed / value_definition
       Q: Which specific instantiation of 'delivered value' should the optimiser use — OEE-weighted throughput (parts × availability × quality rate), revenue-per-shift (£/shift), or parts-per-resource-hour — given that these can rank schedules differently when quality loss and machine downtime trade off against raw output speed?
       A: Use OEE-weighted throughput (good-parts produced × OEE score) as the value numerator, as it is the most universally available metric and penalises quality defects that inflate raw throughput numbers.
    3. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed / lower_bound (missing)
       Q: Is there a minimum acceptable level of output or throughput below which the shift is considered a failure regardless of how efficient the ratio looks — for instance, must at least 80% of the planned production order be completed before the shift ends?
       A: Enforce a soft floor requiring delivered value ≥ 85% of the shift production plan target; if no plan target is available, floor defaults to the 10th-percentile historical shift output so the optimiser cannot achieve a high ratio via near-zero activity.
    4. operator_fatigue_index / intra-shift recovery model
       Q: Does the cumulative ergonomic fatigue index reset or partially recover during scheduled breaks (e.g. a 30-minute mid-shift break), or is it strictly monotonically accumulating across the full shift — because if recovery is allowed, the optimiser may legally schedule high-intensity bursts separated by breaks, which may not match management intent?
       A: Treat fatigue as strictly cumulative with no recovery within the shift (conservative interpretation); additionally enforce that fatigue must remain ≤ 0.4 at every sampling interval, not just at shift end, so the constraint is a running ceiling rather than a terminal value.
    5. ambient_noise_level / LAeq averaging window
       Q: Over what time window is the 80 dB(A) LAeq computed — per individual task cycle, per hour, or across the full shift — since a 15-minute burst at 92 dB averaged into an 8-hour shift LAeq could fall below 80 dB yet still exceed EU Directive 2003/10/EC short-term exposure limits that also require a peak cap of 135 dB(C)?
       A: Apply the 80 dB(A) limit as a rolling 1-hour LAeq window (most conservative common industrial interpretation) and additionally enforce a C-weighted instantaneous peak ceiling of 135 dB(C) at any moment during the shift to comply with the full scope of EU Directive 2003/10/EC.

